Minister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION      (Shri Murlidhar Mohol)
(a): AAI's positive performance assessment under the MoU framework is attributed not only to major projects undertaken, but is also owing to its achievement against key financial, operational, and governance parameters. In FY 2023-24, AAI achieved Rs.14,962.76 crore as 'Revenue from Operations' against the target of Rs.14,000 crore. Additionally, AAI recorded its highest-ever Capital Expenditure of Rs.5,335.00 crore in FY 2023-24, reflecting its commitment to Airport development.;
;
The major projects completed by AAI during the FY 2023-24 include the inauguration of new Terminal Buildings, development of allied infrastructure and new Airport at Chennai, Port Blair, Surat, Tiruchirapally, Ayodhya, Tezu, Pune, Kolhapur, Gwalior, Jabalpur, Rajkot, Aligarh, Azamgarh, Chithrakoot, Moradabad, Shravasti Airports respectively.
;
AAI also  endeavored  to meet its key MoU targets and mandates in line with Government of India directives, including Government e-Marketplace (GeM) Procurement, Trade Receivables Discounting System (TReDS) Portal implementation, Corporate Governance, and Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) initiatives, further reinforcing its strong performance evaluation.;
;
(b): AAI is committed to maintaining and further improving its MoU rating in the coming years through proactive performance monitoring and strategic interventions. AAI periodically reviews all MoU parameters to ensure alignment with targeted levels. Additionally, best efforts are made to ensure compliance with Government-mandated parameters, thereby reinforcing AAI's commitment to operational excellence and governance.;
;
(c): Government of India (GoI) has formulated a Greenfield Airports (GFA) Policy, 2008 for development of new Greenfield airports in the country. As per the policy, if any Airport developer including the State Government desires to develop an airport, they are required to identify a suitable site and get the Prefeasibility study conducted for construction of airport and submit a proposal to the Central Government for 'Site Clearance' followed by 'In-Principle' approval.;
GoI has not received any proposal seeking its approval under Greenfield Airports Policy, 2008 for construction of a Greenfield Airport at Palghar, Maharashtra either from the State Government of Maharashtra or any Airport Developer.

THE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE
(SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV)
(a) to (f): The Food Safety and Standards Authority of India (FSSAI), as the Food regulator of the country, ensures food safety of imported food items. FSSAI has its own Food Import Clearance System (FICS), which is an advanced paperless digital platform integrated with the Customs' ICE-GATE (Indian Customs Electronic Commerce/Electronic Data Interchange (EC/EDI) Gateway) under SWIFT (Single window interface for facilitating trade).
In order to regulate food import with the objective of ensuring food safety, FSSAI has notified Authorised Officers (AO) at a total of 162 food import entry points [Airports/Sea Ports/Inland Container Depots (ICDs) /Special Economic Zones (SEZs)/ Land Customs Stations(LCSs)].
As per the provisions of Food Safety and Standards (Import) Regulations, 2017, FSSAI launched Food Import Rejection Alert (FIRA) portal in September 2024. ‘FIRA’ serves the purpose of an online Alert notification platform for both general public and Food Safety Authority of exporting country, which is also integrated with the Food Import Clearance System (FICS 2.0) of FSSAI. This portal provides information regarding the rejection of imported food items from exporting countries to India. Therefore, such rejected food items do not enter the Indian territory including Jharkhand. This Portal has several benefits: -
Rapid exchange of information amongst food authorities worldwide on rejected imported food items in India;
Enables relevant food authorities/stakeholders to take corrective action for prevention & control any food related in their country;
Online interactive interface for rapid dissemination of information;
Serves as a valuable database for tracking rejected food products and further strengthening of the Risk Management System (RMS).
The details of Food Import Rejection Data is available in public domain on the FIRA portal at fira.fssai.gov.in.
